  "result": "Solving Spatial Supersensing Without Spatial Supersensing (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.16655v1)\nciteturn6754view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6748view3\",\"lineno\":491}); Total lines: 537\nL459@P9: datasets are multi-dimensional. arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.23499, 2025. 8\nL463@P9: Askell, Pamela Mishkin, Jack Clark, et al. Learning transferable visual models from natural language supervision. In\nL464@P9: International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), 2021. 3, 5\nL465@P9-10: 10Krishna Kumar Singh, Kayvon Fatahalian, and Alexei A Efros. Krishnacam: Using a longitudinal, single-person, egocentric\nL466@P10: dataset for scene understanding tasks. In 2016 IEEE Winter Conference on Applications of Computer Vision (WACV),\nL467@P10: pages 1–9. IEEE, 2016. 8\nL468@P10: Enxin Song, Wenhao Chai, Guanhong Wang, Yucheng Zhang, Haoyang Zhou, Feiyang Wu, Haozhe Chi, Xun Guo, Tian Ye,\nL469@P10: Yanting Zhang, et al. Moviechat: From dense token to sparse memory for long video understanding. In CVPR, 2024. 5\nL470@P10: Matthias Tangemann, Matthias K¨ummerer, Thomas SA Wallis, and Matthias Bethge. Measuring the importance of temporal\nL471@P10: features in video saliency. In European Conference on Computer Vision, pages 667–684. Springer, 2020. 2\nL472@P10: Vishaal Udandarao, Mehdi Cherti, Shyamgopal Karthik, Jenia Jitsev, Samuel Albanie, and Matthias Bethge. A good\nL473@P10: crepe needs more than just sugar: Investigating biases in compositional vision-language benchmarks. arXiv preprint\nL474@P10: arXiv:2506.08227, 2025. 8\nL475@P10: Jihan Yang, Shusheng Yang, Anjali W Gupta, Rilyn Han, Li Fei-Fei, and Saining Xie. Thinking in space: How multimodal\nL476@P10: large language models see, remember, and recall spaces. In Proceedings of the Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL477@P10: Conference, pages 10632–10643, 2025a. 6\nL478@P10: Shusheng Yang, Jihan Yang, Pinzhi Huang, Ellis Brown, Zihao Yang, Yue Yu, Shengbang Tong, Zihan Zheng, Yifan Xu,\nL479@P10: Muhan Wang, et al. Cambrian-s: Towards spatial supersensing in video. arXiv preprint arXiv:2511.04670, 2025b. 2, 6, 8\nL480@P10: Jian Yao, Ran Cheng, and Kay Chen Tan. Var-math: Probing true mathematical reasoning in llms via symbolic multi-instance\nL481@P10: benchmarks. arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.12885, 2025. 8\nL482@P10: Mert Yuksekgonul, Federico Bianchi, Pratyusha Kalluri, Dan Jurafsky, and James Zou. When and why vision-language\nL483@P10: models behave like bag-of-words models, and what to do about it? arXiv preprint arXiv:2210.01936, 2022. 5\nL484@P10: Xiaohua Zhai, Basil Mustafa, Alexander Kolesnikov, and Lucas Beyer. Sigmoid loss for language image pre-training. In\nL485@P10: Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision, pages 11975–11986, 2023. 3\nL486@P10: Haoji Zhang, Yiqin Wang, Yansong Tang, Yong Liu, Jiashi Feng, Jifeng Dai, and Xiaojie Jin. Flash-vstream: Memory-based\nL487@P10: real-time understanding for long video streams. arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.08085, 2024. 5\nL488@P10: Daniil Zverev, Thadd¨aus Wiedemer, Ameya Prabhu, Matthias Bethge, Wieland Brendel, and A Koepke. Vggsounder:\nL489@P10: Audio-visual evaluations for foundation models. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer\nL490@P10: Vision, pages 1027–1037, 2025. 8\nL491@P10-11: 11A Feedback from Cambrian-S Authors\nL492@P11: We aim to foster open discussion in the spirit of journals such as Behavioral and Brain Sciences (BBS), which use\nL493@P11: an open peer commentary format for controversial work. Before releasing this work, we emailed the Cambrian-S\nL494@P11: authors to share our findings. We present their response to give readers a more balanced perspective on this work:\nL495@P11: Response from the Cambrian-S authors (November 20th, 2025)\nL496@P11: Thank you very much for contacting us and sharing your findings! We truly appreciate the attention you have given\nL497@P11: to our work. Below, we have answered your questions, giving you our different views and some personal thoughts.\nL498@P11: After thoroughly reading your manuscript, we find your experimental settings to be sound. Some of your test setups\nL499@P11: are exactly what we had in mind! We appreciate your effort in designing this simple yet strong solution and sharing\nL500@P11: it with us.\nL501@P11: However, we would like to provide our different view as below:\nL502@P11: First, we respectfully disagree the strong performance of NoSense implies VSR is ill-suited for\nL503@P11: supersensing.\nL504@P11: A benchmark is always designed with specific purpose, to gauge specific capabilities or measure progress. However,\nL505@P11: once released, a benchmark can often be “solved” in ways unintended by the design. The meaning of a result depends\nL506@P11: not only on the benchmark’s design purpose but also on how it is used.\nL507@P11: VSR is like a “Needle-In-A-Haystack” (NIAH) task for video. In the NLP world, NIAH tests a language model’s\nL508@P11: ability to understand very long texts by hiding a specific “needle” (like a random hash) in a long document. A\nL509@P11: trivial solution to solve this is to use a search tool (like CTRL+F). But NIAH is designed to evaluate the LLM’s\nL510@P11: attention in long context, not a truly hard problem in computer science or NLP. Most modern language models\nL511@P11: can easily solve this problem by writing code, however, we cannot claim that the resulting perfect performance in\nL512@P11: that way equates to perfect long-context understanding or the benchmark fails to achieve its purpose.\nL513@P11: Similarly, your solution perfectly solves any Video NIAH task. But this actually diverges from the intended purpose\nL514@P11: and expected usage of VSI-SUPER. Our goal is to build a smart MLLM that can handle long memories, not a specific\nL515@P11: tool that only works perfectly for “video needle-in-the-haystack”.\nL516@P11: Also, it is noteworthy that we did think about this simple kind of solution when we designed VSR. That is why\nL517@P11: we assume the model cannot know the search query while watching the video. This is closer to how human\nL518@P11: memory and unconscious inference work: we notice and remember things without a specific question, and then we\nL519@P11: recall them only when a specific question is asked. Your email is a very helpful reminder that we did not explain this\nL520@P11: point clearly in our paper. We will fix this and highlight it as soon as possible.\nL521@P11: Second, about the VSC-Repeat failure, we know this is a limitation of our Cambrian-S (w/ Surprise Seg) model.\nL522@P11: While this might sound like an excuse, we actually chose not to include repeated scenes in the VSC design at first.\nL523@P11: Including repeats is more realistic but makes finding a strong and general solution much harder. Your email is a\nL524@P11: great reminder that we probably still need to add this setup to the next iteration of VSC.\nL525@P11: We truly appreciate your effort and for sharing your observations with us!\nL526@P11: As we stated in our paper, Cambrian-S and VSI-SUPER are just the first step toward spatial supersensing. Our\nL527@P11: current model and method are not yet ready for the complexities of the real world, and the benchmark is still limited\nL528@P11: compared to the full scope of spatial supersensing.\nL529@P11: We completely agree that we must do more to fix biases and make the benchmark more realistic. We are now working\nL530@P11: to gather more real-world, long video data (not just joined short clips) to build a better set of tests. We are also\nL531@P11: trying to improve our models to have better spatial sensing ability.\nL532@P11: Finally, thank you again for pointing out the current limitations in both our benchmark and our\nL533@P11: model.\nL534@P11: We’d be excited to see your findings published on arXiv, and we think it would be even stronger if you could include\nL535@P11: our response in the appendix. That way readers have the complete context.\nL536@P11: 12\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLlamazip: Leveraging LLaMA for Lossless Text Compression and Training Dataset Detection (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17589v1)\nciteturn6754view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view2\",\"lineno\":129}); Total lines: 406\nL97: The work presented in this Section also examines the effects of LLM quantization and context window size on the performance of the NTP task. Quantization is a technique that reduces the size of the parameters and can potentially speed up both inference and training by simplifying calculations, although it can also result in a decrease in the precision of the parameters.\nL98: The context window refers to the number of tokens considered when predicting the next token; a larger window requires more memory and longer computation times. Analyzing the impact of these two variables reveals performance variations in Llamazip, thereby reflecting the performance of the underlying model.\nL99: ### 4.1 Options\nL100: \nL101: The model has several options that affect the efficiency of the algorithm, measured by the compression ratio. The exact parameters used to compress a file must be known at the decompression time to retrieve the original data.\nL102: \nL103:   * •\nL104: Quantization: Recent advances in quantization allow for a reduction in the memory requirement of large models, at the cost of a decrease in the compression ratio. Different quantizations are compared: float16, bfloat16, int8 and int4, each representing different levels of precision. Float32 (no quantization) was not tested, as Llama3 cannot fit on the available hardware.\nL105: \nL106:   * •\nL107: Maximum Window Size: Although the compression ratio would probably be maximized by using all previous tokens to predict the next token, the size of the available memory restricts the number of tokens that can be exploited for NTP. Although the window size can take any value in $\\mathbb{N}^{*}$, it was restricted to the set [16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048] in the context of the current work, since each additional token increases the memory requirements of the algorithm.\nL108: ### 4.2 Algorithm\nL109: \nL110: In this section, we present a detailed explanation of Llamazip, a lossless text compression algorithm. The algorithm is designed to efficiently reduce the size of a text file, while ensuring that the original data can be fully recovered without any loss of information.\nL111: #### 4.2.1 Compression\nL112: \nL113: Compression relies mainly on LLaMA3’s predictive capacity, since once a token is correctly predicted, there is no need to record it, and therefore there is only a need to record errors.\nL114: \nL115:   1. 1.\nL116: \nL117: The counter is initialised with the value 0.\nL118: \nL119:   2. 2.\nL120: \nL121: The text to compress is tokenized.\nL122: \nL123:   3. 3.\nL124: \nL125: The first 10 tokens are added to a list and saved directly at the beginning of the output file.\nL126: \nL127:   4. 4.\nL128: The model tries to predict the next token from the tokens stored in the list until the file is fully compressed. There are two possibilities:\nL129: \nL130:      * •\nL131: \nL132: If the prediction is successful, the value of the counter is incremented, and the predicted token is appended to the list.\nL133: \nL134:      * •\nL135: If the prediction is wrong, the counter is recorded in the output file and then set to 0. The index of the token, preceded by the character “@” to distinguish it from the value of the counter, is recorded in the output file and appended to the list.\nL136: \nL137:   5. 5.\nL138: \nL139: After each prediction, the size of the list is examined, and once again there are two possibilities:\nL140: \nL141:      * •\nL142: \nL143: If the size of the list is less than the maximum window size, no further operations are required.\nL144: \nL145:      * •\nL146: If the size of the list is equal to the maximum window size, the oldest value of the list is discarded.\nL147: \nL148:   6. 6.\nL149: \nL150: Once all the data have been read, the counter value is written for the last time to the end of the file.\nL151: \nL152:   7. 7.\nL153: \nL154: The file is then further compressed using gzip.\nL155: #### 4.2.2 Decompression\nL156: \nL157: Decompression involves following the pattern recorded during compression, i.e. alternating between prediction sequences and recovery sequences for tokens written as they are in the file.\nL158: \nL159:   1. 1.\nL160: \nL161: The file is first decompressed using gzip.\nL162: \nL163:   2. 2.\nL164: \nL165: The first 10 tokens are added to a list.\nL166: \nL167:   3. 3.\nL168: \nL169: The next value is read. There are two possibilities:\nL170: \nL171:      * •\nL172: If the first character of the value is “@”, it means that it is an index of a token and not a counter value. The index of the token is appended to the list and to an intermediate file. If the size of the list corresponds to the maximum window size, the oldest token is discarded from the list.\nL173: \nL174:      * •\nL175: Else, the LLaMA3 model predicts $n$ tokens, where $n$ is the value of the counter. After each prediction, the token is added to the list and to the intermediate file, and if required, the oldest token is removed from the beginning of the list.\nL176: \nL177:   4. 4.\nL178: \nL179: The intermediate file is then detokenized in order to retrieve the characters.\nL180: ### 4.3 Similar Work\nL181: LLMZip cite55†Valmeekam et al. (2023) is a compression algorithm that relies on LLaMA1, in its 7 billion parameter version, to compress text data. Similarly to Llamazip, LLMZip relies on the NTP capabilities of a pre-trained LLM to avoid recording every token. However, while Llamazip only registers tokens that were accurately predicted and discards inaccurate hypotheses, LLMZip saves the rank of the correct token.\nL182: In fact, at each step, an LLM can output the probability that each token in the vocabulary is the next token, so it is possible to sort the tokens by probability and record the position of the correct token. In this manner, LLMZip only records integers, where the lowest values mean that the tokens are among the most likely and the highest values mean that the LLM estimated that the token only had a low probability of appearing at this location.\nL183: ## 5 Results\nL184: \nL185: Various text files, all in English, have been compressed using Llamazip. Each file was compressed with 8 different window sizes, as well as 4 different quantization settings, for a total of 32 combinations per file. The sizes and compressions ratios between the optimal Llamazip parameters and different compression algorithms are then compared.\nL186: ### 5.1 Effects of Quantization and Context Window\nL187: \nL188: Figure cite56†1 shows the size of the novel compressed by Llamazip depending on the parameters used. It is immediately apparent that a long context window and quantization that only halves the initial size of the model are factors that improve the compression ratio. Both float16 and bfloat16 display similar efficiencies.\nL189: \nL190: cite57†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Compression of alice29 depending on the context window and the quantization method\nL191: Figure cite58†2 shows the evolution of the size of the Frankenstein novel depending on the quantization and context window. Similarly to the alice29.txt file, the length of the context window and the quantization method have a large impact on the size of the compressed file.\nL192: \nL193: cite59†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Compression of Frankenstein depending on the context window and the quantization method\nL194: The excellent compression ratios of Llamazip for both alice29 and Frankenstein documents are probably due to the fact that these documents were part of the dataset on which the underlying model, LLaMA3, was trained. It is crucial to check the effectiveness of Llamazip on a document more recent than the LLaMA3 release date, as this is the only way to ensure that the document is not part of the training set.\nL195: Following the same pattern as previously observed, Figure cite60†3 demonstrates the impact the context window and quantization method have.\nL196: cite61†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Compression of FSDFSD depending on the context window and the quantization method\nL197: \nL198: Consistently with previous results, Figure cite62†4 shows how a longer compression window with less lossy quantization outperforms other parameters for the LLaMAgen file.\nL199: \nL200: cite63†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: Compression of the LLaMAgen file depending on the context window and the quantization method\nL201: The results obtained for the Mistralgen document are similar to those obtained on LLaMAgen and are shown in Figure cite64†5 .\nL202: \nL203: cite65†Image: Refer to caption Figure 5: Compression of the Mistralgen file depending on the context window and the quantization method\nL204: \nL205: Figures cite66†6 and cite67†7 show the compressed sizes of the MIT1 and MIT2 documents depending on the context window and quantization. The impact of these settings is less significant than for the alice29 and Frankenstein documents.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTime dependent loss reweighting for flow matching and diffusion models is theoretically justified (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16599v1)\nciteturn6754view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view3\",\"lineno\":631}); Total lines: 814\nL578: Note that in contrast to convex combinations, the terms $\\alpha_{i}$ can be negative. For example, if $\\Omega$ contains two distinct points, then $\\mathrm{aff}(\\Omega)$ must contain the line through them.\nL579: ###### Lemma A.3.\nL580: \nL581: Let $\\Omega\\subset V$ be a closed convex subset of $(V,\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle)$, and let $\\phi:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be a continuous strictly convex function that is differentiable in $\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega)$. Define the Bregman divergence $D_{\\phi}:\\Omega\\times\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega)\\to\\mathbb{R}$ by\nL582: \nL583:  | $$D_{\\phi}(a,b)=\\phi(a)-\\phi(b)-\\langle a-b,\\nabla\\phi(b)\\rangle.$$  |\nL584: Let $(\\Xi,\\mathcal{F},\\mu)$ be a probability space and let $X:\\Xi\\to\\Omega\\subset V$ be a random vector such that $\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[X]\\in\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega)$. Then under the same integrability conditions as Lemma cite63†A.1 , it holds\nL585: \nL586:   1. (a)\nL587: \nL588: $\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[X]$ is the unique minimizer of $y\\mapsto\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[D_{\\phi}(X,y)]$ over $\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega)$.\nL589: \nL590:   2. (b)\nL591: $\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[D_{\\phi}(X,y)]=D_{\\phi}(\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[X],y)+\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[D_{\\phi}(X,\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[X])]$, for all $y\\in\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega)$.\nL592: \nL593:   3. (c)\nL594: \nL595: $D_{\\phi}(x,y)=0\\iff x=y$ and $D_{\\phi}(x,y)>0$ if $x\\neq y$, over $x\\in\\Omega$ and $y\\in\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega)$.\nL596: ###### Proof.\nL597: \nL598: (c) is equivalent to Property 1 in Appendix A of cite38†Banerjee et al. (2005) . (b) follows in entirely the same manner as in Lemma cite63†A.1 , and (a) follows from (b) and (c). ∎\nL599: ###### Corollary A.4.\nL600: \nL601: Let $\\Omega$ be convex and closed, and let $\\phi:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be a strictly convex continuous function, differentiable on $\\widehat{\\Omega}\\in\\{\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega),\\Omega\\}$, as in Section cite11†4.2 , and define the Bregman divergence $D_{\\phi}:\\Omega\\times\\widehat{\\Omega}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ by\nL602: \nL603:  | $$D_{\\phi}(x,y)=\\phi(x)-\\phi(y)-\\langle x-y,\\nabla\\phi(y)\\rangle.$$  |\nL604: \nL605: Let $(\\Xi,\\mathcal{F},\\mu)$ be a probability space and let $X:\\Xi\\to\\Omega\\subset V$ be a random vector such that\nL606:   1. 1.\nL607: \nL608: $\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}\\|X\\|<\\infty$, and if $\\widehat{\\Omega}=\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega)$, then $\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[X]\\in\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega).$\nL609: \nL610:   2. 2.\nL611: \nL612: $\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[D_{\\phi}(X,\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}(X))]<\\infty$.\nL613: \nL614: Then it holds\nL615: \nL616:   1. (a)\nL617: \nL618: $\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[X]$ is the unique minimizer of $y\\mapsto\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[D_{\\phi}(X,y)]$ over $\\widehat{\\Omega}$,\nL619: \nL620:   2. (b)\nL621: \nL622: $\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[D_{\\phi}(X,y)]=D_{\\phi}(\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[X],y)+\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[D_{\\phi}(X,\\mathbb{E}_{\\mu}[X])]$ for all $y\\in\\widehat{\\Omega}$,\nL623:   3. (c)\nL624: \nL625: $D_{\\phi}(x,y)=0\\iff x=y$ and $D_{\\phi}(x,y)>0$ if $x\\neq y$ over $x\\in\\Omega$ and $y\\in\\widehat{\\Omega}$.\nL626: ###### Proof.\nL627: \nL628: If $\\widehat{\\Omega}=\\Omega$, then this is Lemma cite63†A.1 . If $\\widehat{\\Omega}=\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega)$, then this is Lemma cite64†A.3 . ∎\nL629: \nL630: ## Appendix B Examples of Bregman Divergences\nL631: \nL632: Here, we present several examples of Bregman divergences, e.g., as in cite31†Holderrieth et al. (2025) and cite32†Lipman et al. (2024) .\nL633: ###### Example B.1 (Time-Scaled Bregman Divergence).\nL634: \nL635: Let $\\phi_{t}:\\Omega_{t}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be a strictly convex differentiable function from the closed convex set $\\Omega_{t}\\subset V_{t}$ where $(V_{t},\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle_{t})$ is an inner product space. Let $w(t)>0$ and consider the mapping $\\psi_{t}(x)=w(t)\\phi_{t}(x)$. Under these conditions,\nL636:  | $\\displaystyle D_{\\psi_{t}}(y,x)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\psi_{t}(y)-\\psi_{t}(x)-\\langle y-x,\\nabla\\psi_{t}(x)\\rangle$  |\nL637:  |  | $\\displaystyle=w(t)\\Big(\\phi_{t}(y)-\\phi_{t}(x)-\\langle y-x,\\nabla\\phi_{t}(x)\\rangle\\Big)$  |\nL638:  |  | $\\displaystyle=w(t)D_{\\phi_{t}}(y,x).$  |\nL639: The above shows that $w(t)D_{\\phi_{t}}(y,x)$ defines a Bregman divergence for each $t\\in[0,1]$. A critical example is when $w(t)\\equiv C>0$, some positive constant, which in particular shows that a Bregman divergence rescaled by a positive constant is still a Bregman divergence.\nL640: ###### Example B.2 (MSE).\nL641: \nL642: Let $\\phi_{\\mathrm{MSE}}:\\mathbb{R}^{N}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be the strictly convex and differentiable function $\\phi_{\\mathrm{MSE}}(x)=\\|x\\|^{2}=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}x^{i}x^{i}$, using superscripts for components. Then the Bregman divergence associated to $\\phi_{\\mathrm{MSE}}$ is given by\nL643:  | $\\displaystyle D_{\\mathrm{MSE}}(y,x)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\phi_{\\mathrm{MSE}}(y)-\\phi_{\\mathrm{MSE}}(x)-\\langle y-x,\\nabla\\phi_{\\mathrm{MSE}}(x)\\rangle$  |\nL644:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}(y^{i}y^{i}-x^{i}x^{i}-2(y^{i}-x^{i})(x^{i}))=\\|y-x\\|^{2}.$  |\nL645: ###### Example B.3 (Separable Bregman Divergences).\nL646: Let $\\phi_{i}:\\Omega_{i}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be strictly convex and differentiable functions for $i=1,\\ldots,N$. Suppose further that $\\Omega_{i}\\subset V_{i}$ is convex and closed, where $(V_{i},\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle_{i})$ is an inner product space. Consider $V=\\bigoplus_{i=1}^{N}V_{i}$ and $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^{N}\\Omega_{i}$, and let the inner product on $V$ be given by $\\langle y,x\\rangle=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\langle y^{i},x^{i}\\rangle_{i}$, using superscripts to denote the $i$’th-coordinate.\nL647: Notice that $\\Omega$ is convex and closed, and define $\\Phi:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb{R}$ by $\\Phi(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\phi_{i}(x^{i})$. Then $\\Phi$ is strictly convex and differentiable. This gives rise to a separable Bregman divergence, where $D_{\\phi_{1}},\\ldots,D_{\\phi_{N}}$ act separately on each of the $N$-components:\nL648:  | $\\displaystyle D_{\\Phi}(y,x)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\Phi(y)-\\Phi(x)-\\langle y-x,\\nabla\\Phi(x)\\rangle$  |\nL649:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\Big(\\phi_{i}(y^{i})-\\phi_{i}(x^{i})-\\langle y^{i}-x^{i},\\nabla\\phi_{i}(x^{i})\\rangle_{i}\\Big)$  |\nL650:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}D_{\\phi_{i}}(y^{i},x^{i}).$  |\nL651: ###### Remark B.4.\nL652: \nL653: There is a natural analogue to Example cite53†B.3 when each Bregman divergence acts on the relative interior via $D_{\\phi_{i}}:\\Omega_{i}\\times\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega_{i})\\to\\mathbb{R}$, since $\\mathrm{ri}(\\prod_{i=1}^{n}\\Omega_{i})=\\prod_{i=1}^{n}\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega_{i})$, noting that each acting on the relative interior should not be particularly restrictive on the class of loss functions since $\\mathrm{ri}(V_{i})=V_{i}$.\nL654: ###### Remark B.5.\nL655: \nL656: In the next two examples, we show two Bregman divergences up to a constant in the $y$, i.e. the left slot, since the model is passed to the right slot. We also consider Bregman divergences as mappings $D:\\Omega\\times\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega)\\to\\mathbb{R}$ in these examples, as discussed in Section cite11†4.2 and Lemma cite64†A.3 . Note that $\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega)$ is the interior of $\\Omega$ within the affine hull of $\\Omega$ — this is discussed in more detail in Remark cite40†A.2 .\nL657: ###### Example B.6 (Poisson-Style Bregman).\nL658: \nL659: Let $\\phi_{\\mathrm{Poiss}}:[0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be given by\nL660: \nL661:  | $$\\phi_{\\mathrm{Poiss}}(x)=\\begin{cases}x\\log x&\\text{if}\\quad x\\in(0,\\infty),\\\\\nL662: 0&\\text{if}\\quad x=0.\\end{cases}$$  |\nL663: \nL664: A computation shows that $\\phi$ is strictly convex and continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and differentiable on $\\mathrm{ri}([0,\\infty))=(0,\\infty)$. Its associated Bregman divergence is $D_{\\mathrm{Poiss}}:[0,\\infty)\\times(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb{R}$,\nL665:  | $$D_{\\mathrm{Poiss}}(y,x)=y\\log y-x\\log x-(y-x)(1+\\log x)=x-y\\log x+f(y),$$  |\nL666: \nL667: where $f(y)=y\\log y-y$ is a function only of $y$.\nL668: ###### Example B.7 (Binary Cross Entropy).\nL669: \nL670: Let $\\phi_{\\mathrm{BCE}}:[0,1]\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be given by\nL671: \nL672:  | $$\\phi_{\\mathrm{BCE}}(x)=\\begin{cases}x\\log x+(1-x)\\log(1-x)&\\text{if}\\quad x\\in(0,1)\\\\\nL673: 0&\\text{if}\\quad x\\in\\{0,1\\}.\\end{cases}$$  |\nL674: \nL675: Then $\\phi$ is strictly convex and continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $\\mathrm{ri}([0,1])=(0,1)$. Its associated Bregman divergence is $D_{\\mathrm{BCE}}:[0,1]\\times(0,1)\\to\\mathbb{R}$,\nL676:  | $\\displaystyle D_{\\mathrm{BCE}}(y,x)$  | $\\displaystyle=D_{\\mathrm{Poiss}}(y,x)+D_{\\mathrm{Poiss}}(1-y,1-x)$  |\nL677:  |  | $\\displaystyle=-[y\\log x+(1-y)\\log(1-x)]+g(y),$  |\nL678: \nL679: where $g(y)=y\\log(y)+(1-y)\\log(1-y)$.\nL680: ## Appendix C Regularity conditions inherited from Generator Matching\nL681: \nL682: We repeat a few basic definitions from Generator Matching (GM) (cite31†Holderrieth et al., 2025 ) and their regularity conditions. The infinitesimal generator $\\mathcal{L}_{t}$ of a process $X_{t}$ acts on a test function $f\\in\\mathcal{T}$ via\nL683: \nL684:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{t}f(x)=\\frac{d}{dh}\\Bigg|_{h=0}\\Big(\\mathbb{E}[f(X_{t+h})|X_{t}=x]\\Big),$$  |\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.16655v1)\nciteturn6754view3 "
}
